WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:01.957
- The U.S. government
is expected to borrow

00:00:01.957 --> 00:00:05.220
$2 trillion a year for the next decade.

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:07.980
That's huge and unprecedented.

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:09.210
Investors are starting to think

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:12.060
that all this borrowing
could have some major impacts

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:15.210
on stocks, bonds, and all
kinds of other investments.

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:17.370
I'm going to explain why
the government is borrowing

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:21.210
so much money, why all that
borrowing will impact markets,

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:23.550
and what this could mean
for the investing world

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:25.080
in the near future.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:26.160
Let's get into it.

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:29.970
The U.S. national debt
recently hit $33 trillion,

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:31.860
and the Congressional
Budget Office estimates

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:35.610
our debt could surpass $40
trillion in five years.

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:38.220
That's because even though the
government isn't rolling out

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:40.080
any big new stimulus programs,

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:42.270
it is bringing in a lot less revenue

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:44.400
than it did over the past two years.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:47.190
Revenue is basically shorthand for taxes.

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:49.140
The government is collecting less taxes

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:51.540
because people are
collecting less capital gains

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:54.810
on the sale of things like
stocks, businesses, and houses.

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:56.700
Remember, you pay taxes

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:59.250
in the year following taxable events.

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:02.550
So the taxes from big stock
and housing market gains

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:07.550
we saw in 2021 were largely
recorded in fiscal year 2022.

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:10.500
The government is also
paying a lot more in interest

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:13.320
so it can borrow money
to pay for the deficit.

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:15.600
The interest rate on
two-year government bonds

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:19.560
at the beginning of 2022 was less than 1%.

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:22.050
Now that rate is over 5%.

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:23.820
When you're paying interest on billions

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:27.690
or trillions of dollars in
debt, that difference adds up.

00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:30.000
Jefferies U.S. economist Thomas Simons

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.480
is expecting the government to
issue just under $4 trillion

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:36.780
in medium and long-term
debt in fiscal year 2024,

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:39.630
and it's going to have to sell
that debt at the same time

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:42.120
that some of the largest
buyers are cutting back.

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:45.240
The Federal Reserve is the
largest holder of treasury bonds,

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:47.190
but the U.S. Central Bank is projected

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:48.300
to reduce its holdings

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:51.510
by up to $60 billion per
month moving forward.

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:53.610
And the latest report from
the Treasury Department

00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:56.610
shows China cut its holdings
of U.S. government bonds

00:01:56.610 --> 00:01:59.190
to the lowest level since May 2009.

00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:02.430
China was the largest foreign
holder of U.S. government debt

00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:04.320
as recently as 2019

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:08.340
and held more than $1 trillion
in treasuries in early 2021.

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:10.020
But the country has reduced its holdings

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:12.600
by almost $300 billion since then.

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:16.020
When there's more supply and less demand,

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:18.480
that usually means that prices fall.

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:21.780
For bonds, it also means that yields rise.

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:23.670
Treasury yields reached
their highest level

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:26.190
in more than 15 years in September,

00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:28.230
and the mountain of new debt that's coming

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:29.550
could push them even higher.

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:32.970
Higher yields historically
increase the value of the dollar

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:35.190
and boost the attractiveness
of safe assets

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:36.750
like money market funds.

00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:38.940
Retail traders and
professional money managers

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:42.210
have pushed a record amount of
cash into money market funds

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:45.120
which are paying the highest
interest rates in decades.

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:47.370
Higher yields also have made investments

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:50.760
in risky assets like
stocks less attractive.

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:54.870
The thinking goes that if you
can get a guaranteed 5% return

00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:57.630
on an asset that's a lot
like a savings account,

00:02:57.630 --> 00:02:59.490
why take the risk on stocks?

00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:02.100
And because most public
companies have to borrow money

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:04.680
by issuing bonds, their costs will go up,

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:06.840
which often makes them less profitable.

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:10.050
This is especially true for
so-called growth stocks.

00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:11.460
These are usually tech companies

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:13.680
that have very high market valuations,

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:15.930
even though they don't
make a lot of profit.

00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:18.180
Tech has made up an
increasingly large share

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:20.910
of the benchmark S&amp;P 500 stock index

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:23.130
and is the backbone of the Nasdaq.

00:03:23.130 --> 00:03:25.380
With the outlook for
the economy uncertain,

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:26.970
you could see more investors

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:29.970
start to favor parking their
money with the U.S. government

00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:32.362
or leaving it in money market funds.

00:03:32.362 --> 00:03:34.945
(gentle music)

